-- title:
--   Specht modules as irreducible subrepresentations of the tabloid module
-- statement:
--   This file defines the Specht module of a Young diagram as the subrepresentation of the tabloid permutation module generated by the polytabloid, and proves that it is an irreducible representation of the symmetric group on the cells.
--
--   **Setting.** Throughout, $\mu$ is a Young diagram, regarded as a finite set $[\mu]\subset\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}$ of cells $(i,j)$ (row $i$, column $j$, both counted from $0$) that is closed under decreasing either coordinate. Write $\operatorname{row}(i,j)=i$ and $\operatorname{col}(i,j)=j$, let $\mu_i$ be the length of row $i$, and put $n=|[\mu]|$. The cells serve both as positions in the diagram and as the $n$ letters permuted by the symmetric group $\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$. A *tabloid* of shape $\mu$ is a map $r:[\mu]\to\mathbb{N}$ of the form $r=\operatorname{row}\circ p$ for some $p\in\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$: it places each letter $x$ in row $r(x)$, and row $i$ receives exactly $\mu_i$ letters. Let $\mathrm{Tab}(\mu)$ be the (finite) set of tabloids. The *canonical tabloid* is $r_0=\operatorname{row}$ (each letter sits in its own row), and a permutation $g$ acts on tabloids by $g\cdot r=r\circ g^{-1}$. The *column group* $C_\mu$ is the subgroup of permutations $c\in\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$ with $\operatorname{col}(c(x))=\operatorname{col}(x)$ for every cell $x$, i.e. those that move each letter only within its own column. $M^\mu$ is the space of complex functions on $\mathrm{Tab}(\mu)$ with the inner product $\langle f,h\rangle=\sum_{r}\overline{f(r)}\,h(r)$ and the unitary representation $(\rho_\mu(g)f)(r)=f(g^{-1}\cdot r)$ of $\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$; $\delta_r\in M^\mu$ is the indicator function of the single tabloid $r$; $A_\mu=\sum_{c\in C_\mu}\operatorname{sgn}(c)\,\rho_\mu(c)$ is the column antisymmetrizer (with $\operatorname{sgn}$ the sign of a permutation, as a complex number); and $e_\mu=A_\mu\delta_{r_0}$ is the polytabloid.
--
--   1. **Specht module.**
--   $$
--   S^\mu=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{C}}\{\rho_\mu(g)\,e_\mu : g\in\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])\}\subseteq M^\mu ,
--   $$
--   which is a subrepresentation of $M^\mu$, i.e. stable under every $\rho_\mu(g)$.
--
--   2. **Generation.** $e_\mu\in S^\mu$, and $S^\mu\subseteq W$ for every subrepresentation $W$ of $M^\mu$ that contains $e_\mu$.
--
--   3. **Stability under the antisymmetrizer.** Every subrepresentation $W$ of $M^\mu$ satisfies $A_\mu f\in W$ for all $f\in W$.
--
--   4. **Detection.** If $f\in S^\mu$ and $f\neq0$, then $\langle e_\mu,\rho_\mu(g)f\rangle\neq0$ for some $g\in\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$.
--
--   5. **Minimality.** If $W$ is a subrepresentation of $M^\mu$ with $W\subseteq S^\mu$, then $W=0$ or $W=S^\mu$.
--
--   6. **Irreducibility.** $S^\mu$, with the restricted action of $\mathrm{Sym}([\mu])$, is an irreducible representation: it is nonzero and its only subrepresentations are $0$ and $S^\mu$ itself.
--
--   For every Young diagram with $n$ cells this gives an explicit irreducible complex representation of the symmetric group on $n$ letters, with no hypothesis on $\mu$. Together with the non-isomorphism of Specht modules of different shapes and the bound of the number of irreducible representations by the number of conjugacy classes, it identifies the irreducible representations of $S_n$ with the Young diagrams with $n$ cells.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $S^\mu$ is `spechtSub μ` (a `Subrepresentation (tabloidRep μ)`), and `spechtRep μ` is the representation on its underlying submodule. Irreducibility is Mathlib's `Representation.IsIrreducible`, registered as the instance `specht_irreducible`; items 2 to 5 are `polytabloid_mem_specht`, `specht_le_of_polytabloid_mem`, `antisym_mem_invariant`, `specht_detect` and `specht_minimal`.
